  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1231 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nIt is a beautiful fall day, thanks to the weak synoptic descent\namidst northwest flow aloft, with a digging trough over the \nGreat Lakes region and a weak ridge building over the Rocky \nMountains. Clear skies, aside from some high- level cirrus \nclouds potentially drifting into the northern potions of the \narea, and modest dew points in the upper 30s to low 40s will \nallow high temperatures to climb into the upper 60s to low 70s \nthis afternoon. Calm winds will gradually become more southerly \nthis afternoon and evening as a high pressure center in the \nSoutheast continues to dictate the low-level flow.\n\nFog is again possible tonight into tomorrow morning, as weak winds \novernight combine with efficient radiative cooling. Recent REFS \nguidance currently depicts a 30-60% chance of visibilities dropping \nbelow 1 mile tomorrow morning, with this fog potential mainly confined \nto low-lying areas along and south of Highway 136. Low temperatures are \nexpected to fall into the upper 30s to low 40s by tomorrow morning. \n\nThe warm, dry weather is expected to continue through the start of the \nwork week, as the flow aloft remains west-northwesterly. Winds will \ngenerally be out of the west to northwest tomorrow afternoon, helping \nsupport high temperatures peaking into the upper 60s to low 70s. Fog is \nagain possible (roughly 40-70% chance) tomorrow night into Tuesday \nmorning. However, its development will be hindered by the westerly \nsurface flow, potentially reducing dew points low enough to not reach \nsaturation despite the efficient radiation cooling under the clear \nskies (similar to what happened last night). A shortwave trough will \npass over the region Monday evening through Tuesday, supporting an area \nof low pressure to the east of the area. While this wave is not \nexpected to bring precipitation to the area, it will bring high \ntemperatures back into the 60s Tuesday and Wednesday. Wednesday will \nsee gusty northerly winds in the wake of a cold front Tuesday night.\n\nOverall, models are in general agreement that a ridge will develop over \nthe Intermountain West and a trough feature will reside over the \nMidwest/Ohio Valley. However, there is still some disagreement in there \nthese features set up, causing a large temperature distribution in both \nthe NBM and LREF. The GEFS (and parent GFS recent run) still \nplace this trough slightly more to the west, with more \nshortwaves embedded within this flow, supporting the cooler end \nof the range (highs in the low 50s). On the contrary, the ENS \n(and the most recent run of its parent ECMWF model) continue to \nshow this trough moving through slightly quicker, placing it \nfurther to the East. This eastward placement would allow the \nwarmer end of the spectrum (highs in the upper 50s).",
